            <ArticleTitle>Prior clopidogrel use and outcome of acute coronary syndrome.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>313-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Prior aspirin use has been associated with poorer outcome in acute coronary syndrome, and forms part of the TIMI Risk Score. It is not known if prior use of clopidogrel is associated with similar risk.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To assess if prior clopidogrel use is associated with higher risk in acute coronary syndrome.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Participants were 869 consecutive admissions to a Scottish district general hospital with suspected acute coronary syndrome.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Incidence of death, recurrent myocardial infarction or urgent percutaneous intervention at 2 weeks was recorded. Odds ratios for sub-groups on clopidogrel, aspirin or neither were calculated.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Odds ratios were: clopidogrel 1.46 (95% ci 0.62-3.33), aspirin 1.09 (95% ci 0.64-1.85), neither 0.91 (95% ci 0.53-1.54).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">No definite association was shown between clopidogrel use and outcome but there was a trend towards increased risk of major acute coronary events.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
